## Problem
By default, no client should get MCP configured unless the user deliberately opts in.

Related completed work: **B122** ("Require explicit MCP opt-in and retire user-facing Python MCP paths") is marked done. This task is a re-verify / residual-enforcement pass: make sure the policy still holds for every client path today (claude, codex, opencode, kimi, grok, agy, pi), including:

- bare `c2c install` (interactive TUI defaults)
- `c2c install all`
- `c2c install <client>` without explicit MCP flags
- `c2c init` / managed start / new paths that might re-write MCP config
- docs/hero copy that might imply MCP is automatic

## Acceptance criteria
- No c2c install/init/start path enables client MCP unless the user makes an explicit opt-in choice.
- Dry-run evidence for each client: `c2c install <client> --dry-run` (and `install all --dry-run`) does not configure MCP by default.
- Plain CLI messaging remains usable without MCP.
- If any residual path still auto-enables MCP, fix it and add a regression test.
- Note any intentional exceptions (e.g. if a client *only* has MCP and has no CLI-only path) explicitly in the task notes.

## Context
Requested by Max after site/onboarding work. Policy north star matches B122: MCP is explicit opt-in only.